<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-asparagine biosynthesis I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="ASPARAGINE-BIOSYNTHESIS" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; In both eukaryotes and prokaryotes, |FRAME: ASN| is biosynthesized from |FRAME: L-ASPARTATE| by amidation using |FRAME: GLN| as an amino group donor. In some organisms, including |FRAME:TAX-83333|, a second pathway exists that utilizes |FRAME: AMMONIA| instead (see |FRAME: ASPARAGINESYN-PWY|). In |FRAME:TAX-83333| |FRAME:ASNSYNB-CPLX|, encoded by |FRAME: EG10092|, catalyzes this pathway, while |FRAME: ASNSYNA-CPLX|, encoded by |FRAME: EG10091|, catalyzes the ammonia-dependent reaction. Null mutations in both genes result in asparagine auxotrophy, as does a null mutation only in |FRAME: EG10092| under conditions of ammonia-limited growth. A null mutation only in |FRAME: EG10091| has no observable phenotype. The mammalian |FRAME: CPLX-7338| uses only cellular |FRAME: GLN| as amide donor. An archael-type asparagine synthetase has been crystallized |CITS: [15039580]|. The only known role of |FRAME: ASN| is incorporation into proteins |CITS: [GOTTSCHALK1986]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="SRI" />
    <Author name="fulcher" />
  </Authors>
